{"content":"Failed-move cleanup can delete another editor's newer destination revision. Cleanup reads and compares a snapshot, then calls deleteItem. If another edit finishes after that read, storage deletion still removes the item by ID.","codeSuggestion":"","assessment":"P1. Recovery can delete changes made by another editor.","rationale":"removePendingMoveCopies separates getItem and deleteItem at CalTransactionManager.sys.mjs:32–34. Storage deleteItem calls deleteItemById without a revision condition. The storage model also deletes by ID. test_moveRollbackPreservesPendingDestinationEdits changes the destination before cleanup reads it, so it does not cover this interval.","recommendation":"change","validation":"An isolated check executed the exact transaction and storage-provider deletion methods with a controlled storage model. Cleanup received revision 1. The destination then changed to revision 2. Deletion removed revision 2. This proves the method sequence with doubles, not SQLite scheduling.","appliedSummary":"Updated cleanup dispatch, four storage modules, and the unit regression test. Cleanup now checks the stored revision during deletion and preserves newer cached items.

Focused lint, syntax, whitespace, 30 SQLite cases, and isolated model/cache checks passed. The xpcshell test remains unrun because the available build has unknown source stamps.

All six changed files remain unstaged and uncommitted.","changeSummary":"Make cleanup deletion conditional on the expected stored revision within the operation that deletes the item. Preserve newer copies. Add coverage for an edit after the cleanup read.","state":"handled","changeAccepted":false,"changeReverted":false,"changesAmended":true}
